.TH UNINSTALL 1 "August 2026" "uninstall 0.19.0"
.SH NAME
uninstall \- find, explain, preview, and safely remove Linux software
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uninstall
[
.B --show-dependencies
] [
.B --json
] [
.B --debug
] [APP]
.br
.B uninstall --self-uninstall
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uninstall
resolves application names, desktop names, commands, package identifiers, and
supported local package archives across native and user-level Linux package
managers. It displays retained installation provenance, uses a native read-only
removal preview where available, optionally selects associated data, repeats and
fingerprints the final preview, and asks for confirmation before changing state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --show-dependencies
Include fuzzy matches installed as dependencies.
.TP
.B --json
Print a read-only machine-readable discovery and preview report. No state is
changed.
.TP
.B --debug
Show per-backend timing and failure diagnostics.
.TP
.B --backend BACKEND --confirm "REMOVE Backend:package-id"
Guarded non-interactive removal for automation. It requires one exact package
identity and refuses blocked, unknown, or high-impact transactions.
.TP
.B --self-uninstall
Remove the installed command itself.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version.
.SH SAFETY
The program must not be run wholesale through sudo. It requests narrowly scoped
privilege only for a system package-manager command. Unknown-impact operations
require a typed confirmation. Associated user data is never selected by default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means cancellation or successful completion. One means no match, blocked
removal, changed preview, failed removal, failed verification, or cleanup error.
Two means invalid command-line usage.
